Both `mcp/github.ts:320` and `:842` are WRITE-side guards over the body the AGENT is composing (pre-write). These are not reads of GitHub-written bodies; they check whether the agent's own draft already contains a tag (the agent shouldn't write its own notify/parent tags). The body is agent-composed text. Hmm — should a fence-quoted notify tag in the agent's draft block the write? The check is "you may not write a notify tag yourself" — a quoted sample in a fence is not the agent writing a tag, it's the agent quoting one. proseOnly would be the more accurate check... but wait, there's a subtlety: proseOnly ignores fenced content, so an agent could hide a real tag in a fence — which is EXACTLY what these guards exist to prevent? No wait — the guard prevents the agent from setting the notify tag; if the agent puts it in a fence, proseOnly would let the write through WITH a tag-shaped literal in a fence... which readers (post-fix) would ignore. So actually proseOnly-ing these write guards ALIGNS with the read side: a tag inside a fence is not a tag. If reads ignore fence tags, then the write guard checking prose is the same rule applied at the other end: "the machinery's tag must be in prose, written by the machinery".

Hmm, but these two are guards on agent-composed text, pre-GitHub. The issue's table says `tools/mcp/github.ts:1758,1763` (current 1806,1811) for "親 Issue、origin agent" — the mergePr reads. It does NOT list 320/842. The write-side guards are a different question ("did the agent try to set this tag itself"). I'll leave 320/842 raw and NOT mention... no, I should mention the boundary in the PR. Actually hold on — if reads become prose-only, then a fence-quoted notify tag in an agent's draft would be refused by the raw `has` guard even though no reader would ever see it as a tag. Is that a false refusal that matters? The agent quoting `<!-- atomaton:notify=bob -->` in a fence in an issue body it creates... the guard refuses the whole create. That's over-strict but safe, and consistent with "the agent may not write this tag" in spirit (why is the agent quoting notify tags?). Leave raw; note it.

Hmm, wait, actually let me reconsider — is it "safe"? The failure mode is a refusal (fail-closed), not a wrong decision. Fine to leave.

Now `countPriorRetries` (validate_pull_request.ts:229): `bodies.filter((body) => !isQuotation(body ?? "") && CI_RETRY_TAG.has(body ?? "")).length`. CI_RETRY_TAG is written by validation's own notices in prose. A person quoting `<!-- atomaton:ci-retry=2 -->` in a fence inflates the count → validation refuses to retry. Same class as AGENT_TAG.has in manage_dispatch_loop. Route through hasBody.

So the final `has`-side changes: DISPATCH_TAG.hasBody (thread.ts isDispatchMarker), AGENT_TAG.hasBody ×2 (manage_dispatch_loop), CI_RETRY_TAG.hasBody (validate_pull_request:229). Leave: QUOTES_TAG (isQuotation, entryOf), NOTIFY_TAG.has/PARENT_ISSUE_TAG.has write-guards in mcp (agent-draft checks, not GitHub reads).

