Now the entry point. Let me write it:

```ts
export interface AtomatonTag<T> {
  readonly marker: string;
  write(value: T): string;
  /** Extract this tag's value from anywhere in `text`, or undefined if absent. */
  read(text: string): T | undefined;
  /**
   * Extract this tag's value from a GitHub body or comment, or undefined if absent.
   * ... proseOnly doc ...
   */
  readBody(text: string): T | undefined;
  /** True if `text` contains this tag at all, regardless of its value. */
  has(text: string): boolean;
  /** True if a GitHub body or comment carries this tag at all, outside fenced code. */
  hasBody(text: string): boolean;
  search(value: T): string;
}
```

Implementation in makeTag:

```ts
return {
  marker: ...,
  write: ...,
  read: (text) => {...},
  readBody: (text) => readFromProse(re, parse, text), // hmm
  has: (text) => re.test(text),
  hasBody: (text) => re.test(proseOnly(text)),
  search: ...,
};
```

Simplest: `readBody: (text) => tag.read(proseOnly(text))` — but inside makeTag, `read` closure isn't named. I can restructure:

```ts
function makeTag<T>(...): AtomatonTag<T> {
  const pattern = ...;
  EVERY_TAG_PATTERN.push(pattern);
  const re = new RegExp(...);
  const read = (text: string): T | undefined => {
    const m = re.exec(text);
    return m ? parse(m[1]!) : undefined;
  };
  return {
    marker: `${TAG_PREFIX}${key}`,
    write: (value) => `<!-- ${TAG_PREFIX}${key}=${render(value)} -->`,
    read,
    // GitHub bodies are read through `proseOnly`: ... (#304)
    readBody: (text) => read(proseOnly(text)),
    has: (text) => re.test(text),
    hasBody: (text) => re.test(proseOnly(text)),
    search: (value) => `${TAG_PREFIX}${key}=${render(value)}`,
  };
}
```

Same for makeCompoundTag.

Naming: `readBody`/`hasBody`? Or `readProse`/`hasProse`? Hmm. The semantics: "read from a GitHub body/comment, ignoring fenced code". `readBody` could be misread as "the body of the tag". `readProse` names the rule (proseOnly). The existing helpers: `proseOnly`, `withoutTags`, `isQuotation`. I think `readProse`/`hasProse` is clearer about WHAT it does; `readBody` names where. The issue says 「GitHub の本文からタグを読むときは proseOnly を通す」— "when reading a tag from a GitHub body, go through proseOnly". Either name satisfies. I'll go with `readProse`/`hasProse`? Hmm... but at call sites, `PARENT_ISSUE_TAG.readProse(body)` reads as "read the parent issue from the prose" — good. vs `readFromBody(PARENT_ISSUE_TAG, body)`.

Hmm, let me think about discoverability for the next writer: they have a body and want the parent. They type `PARENT_ISSUE_TAG.` and see `read`, `readProse`, `has`, `hasProse`. The doc on `read` must say: "Scans anywhere in `text`. For a body or comment as GitHub wrote it, use `readProse` — a tag inside a fenced block there is a sample, not the machinery speaking." That guidance makes the choice obvious.

Decision: `readProse` / `hasProse`. Hmm, wait — or should it be one method with the prose rule and keep `read` for internal? No — issue explicitly says `read` itself staying raw is a separate decision (tests read write() output; internally assembled strings). Keep both.

Actually, hmm, one more name candidate: `readGitHub`/`hasGitHub`? No. `readProse` it is.

Now — the domain interfaces: `TurnReaders`, `AskReaders`, `EndingWalkReaders` take `(body: string) => ...`. The adapter implementations change; no signature change.

One important check: `shapedThread(entries, readers)` — entries' bodies are raw. Readers now read prose. But WAIT — there's an interplay with `entryOf.quoted` and the domain fold: for a QUOTED bot comment, the fold skips it entirely (ending walk). For non-quoted comments, readers now ignore fenced tags. Consistent.

